{
  "body_human_readable": "Archimedes of Syracuse (c. 287 \u2013 c. 212 BCE) was a preeminent ancient Greek mathematician, physicist, engineer, inventor, and astronomer, widely regarded as one of the leading scientists in classical antiquity and one of the greatest mathematicians of all time. Born in the city of Syracuse on the island of Sicily, he made groundbreaking contributions that have had a lasting impact on science and engineering.\n\n### Major Contributions and Discoveries\n\nArchimedes is celebrated for a wide array of achievements across various fields:\n\n**Mathematics:**\n*   **Approximation of Pi (\u03c0):** He was one of the first to derive an accurate approximation of pi, calculating that it lay between 3 1/7 and 3 10/71.\n*   **Advancements in Geometry:** Archimedes made significant discoveries related to the areas, volumes, and surface areas of various geometric shapes. He proved that the volume of a sphere is two-thirds the volume of a cylinder that encloses it, a proof he considered his greatest accomplishment.\n*   **Forerunner to Calculus:** By using concepts like the method of exhaustion, he anticipated integral calculus by 2,000 years, developing methods to calculate areas and volumes.\n*   **Large Numbers:** He devised a system using exponentiation to express very large numbers.\n\n**Physics and Engineering:**\n*   **Archimedes' Principle:** His most famous discovery is the principle of buoyancy, which states that a body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. Legend has it he discovered this while in the bath and ran through the streets naked shouting \"Eureka!\" (\"I have found it!\").\n*   **The Law of the Lever:** He provided a mathematical proof for the law of the lever and is credited with the quote, \"Give me a place to stand, and I will move the Earth.\" He also developed systems of pulleys to move heavy objects with smaller forces.\n*   **The Archimedes Screw:** This invention is a device for raising water, which is still used today for irrigation and in sewage treatment plants. It consists of a screw-shaped surface inside a pipe.\n*   **War Machines:** During the Roman siege of Syracuse, Archimedes designed several \"engines of war\" to defend his city. These included catapults, cranes to drop rocks, and claws to lift ships out of the water. He is also said to have devised a system of mirrors to set enemy ships on fire, though the efficacy of this \"heat ray\" is debated.\n*   **Odometer:** He is credited with designing an early version of an odometer to measure distance.\n\n### Life and Legacy\n\nFew details of Archimedes' life are known for certain. It is believed he studied in Alexandria, Egypt, a major center of learning in the ancient world, before returning to his native Syracuse. He was known to be so engrossed in his work that he would often forget to eat.\n\nArchimedes was killed during the capture of Syracuse by the Romans in 212 or 211 BCE. A Roman soldier reportedly killed him after he refused to leave his mathematical work. The Roman general, Marcus Claudius Marcellus, had admired Archimedes for his inventions and regretted his death. As per his request, his tomb was marked with a sphere enclosed in a cylinder to commemorate his favorite mathematical proof.\n\nHis inventions and mathematical principles have remained influential for millennia, solidifying his reputation as a foundational figure in the history of science.",
